WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.460
Film Courage: Dan how was it for you to get your first
job as a script reader? And if you can

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.780
paint a little bit about your life at
that time?

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:15.330
Daniel Calvisi, Screenwriter: Well I'd gotten out of NYU film and I
definitely was interested in

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:21.359
screenwriting that was kind of my focus
coming out of film school and I really

00:00:21.359 --> 00:00:25.050
wanted to get into script reading and I
had been interning at the Tribeca Film

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.400
Center in New York for a company that
was co-owned by Robert De Niro's

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:35.750
production company and in the same
building was Miramax Films and so I I

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:40.379
just kind of got to know some people
there and it was funny my first reading

00:00:40.379 --> 00:00:45.690
job for them it was their genre division
called dimension films and I remember

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:52.289
calling up the supervisor there or no
I'm sorry I remember getting a referral

00:00:52.289 --> 00:00:57.079
to them but the guy said they're not
hiring now don't bother calling them and

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:03.510
because I was you know I had a lot of
initiative I called anyway and the first

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:08.040
thing the supervisor told me when he
picked up the phone was oh we have a

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.920
reader job open do you want to test for
it and so I did a test coverage which is

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:18.270
they give you a script that they've
already had coverage on and so I did

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:22.710
coverage on it so then they evaluate
they evaluate you on that coverage

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:26.189
because they already know the script
really well and so that's how I got

00:01:26.189 --> 00:01:31.170
working for them and then I transferred
over to the Miramax division which was

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:35.610
their parent company and I had I had
also been freelancing for a number of

00:01:35.610 --> 00:01:41.040
other companies in New York City other
production companies and studios so when

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:46.860
you came to New York to go to NYU do you
remember like some of the scripts in

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:50.700
your mind that you thought this is the
sort of the genre I want to write in

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:57.570
this is what I want to create well I'd
been it was funny because the growing up

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:04.110
I was really kind of more into I guess
like Spielberg stuff or kind of more

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:08.489
action stuff like Rambo and that kind of
stuff but then before I went to NYU I

00:02:08.489 --> 00:02:12.600
transferred in as a junior I started
getting into Scorsese and I was

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:17.010
watching you know taxi driver and that
kind of stuff and Goodfellas and Oliver

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:22.470
Stone and I knew that Scorsese and
Oliver Stone and Spike Lee had gone to

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:28.290
NYU so I really wanted to go there so I
applied and I got in just on the wait

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:34.470
list and then they had called really
late in the summer before my junior year

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:39.170
and said we have an opening on the
waitlist so that's why I went to NYU

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:44.670
that's how I was able to get in and it
was just really cool to be in New York

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:49.950
where these filmmakers that I really
admired had gone you know

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:55.080
so yeah Scorsese Oliver Stone Spike Lee
the Coen brothers and these were all

00:02:55.080 --> 00:03:02.580
filmmakers that I really you know wanted
to emulate so going back to what your

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.840
jobs were what your tasks were so you
finally get in here you were told

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:11.580
sorry don't even bother you give it a
shot anyway it turns out as luck would

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:16.430
have it they do have a slot do you
remember what your first few weeks were

00:03:16.430 --> 00:03:23.160
as the script reader and well I remember
because I got I got scripts and books

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.210
because I was in New York and that was
the literary capitals so I would read a

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:34.590
lot of books ahead of publication they
the agents and book editors would send a

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:39.990
book out to the studios to see if any
studios or production companies wanted

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:44.730
option the movie rights and so I would
be the first reader at the production

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:50.010
company or studio to read that
unpublished galley version and that was

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:53.700
hard to do because a lot of times they
would literally give you a 600 page book

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:59.670
to read overnight and I had to write up
a three to five page report about it

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:06.660
by you know noon the next day and that
was hard to do I actually got I got let

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:11.910
go from one of my jobs early on because
I just I just couldn't turn over the

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:19.200
material that fast and so I had to kind
of learn on the job trial by fire and

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:24.150
just learned to read really fast and to
intuit that structure that movie

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:28.260
structure
and as I'm reading this book I'm asking

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:32.330
myself will this transfer over to a
movie you know does it have that

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:40.260
potential for that 3/4 act structure
that 95% of great movies use and so it

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:44.910
was all about structure there wasn't
also dialogue things ya know it was it

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:49.040
was evaluating the whole thing as a
reader I was evaluating the whole script

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:55.410
structured dialogue you know character
development all that stuff but if you're

00:04:55.410 --> 00:05:01.290
reading something like a novel or a
graphic novel or even like a piece from

00:05:01.290 --> 00:05:06.180
The New Yorker or Rolling Stone or you
know any kind of article that they're

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:11.940
interested in making a movie of you have
to evaluate it as a potential movie so

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:17.460
you're asking yourself does it have the
three or four act structure of a

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:22.380
commercial film so that's interesting so
it sounds actually fairly intimidating

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:25.110
because I know I've been in situations
like that where no one really tells you

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:29.220
what the rules are you kind of have to
as you said into it or watch other

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:33.840
people so how was that where were people
actually giving you tips on how to do

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:37.020
with all that or no you kind of had to
every man for himself

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:40.440
not a whole lot you didn't get a whole
lot of feedback when I was first

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:44.850
starting out maybe a few supervisors
might have given me a few little tips

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:48.360
about coverage but after a while they
don't really give you any feedback you

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:54.240
don't even know where this script goes
after you send in your coverage report

00:05:54.240 --> 00:06:00.120
so I would really have to ask afterward
like hey that that script that I really

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:04.170
loved that anything happened with it and
so they might tell me oh we didn't buy

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:10.020
it but Fox bought it or something like
that but as a reader you you're not told

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.580
anything so you have to inquire you have
to have the initiative to see what

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:18.710
happens after that and you're not a part
of the process of development or

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:22.770
purchasing it or bidding on it or
anything like that after you read it

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:28.380
once maybe twice it's out of your hands
and then your supervisors the

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:34.170
coordinators the execs the VP's they're
the ones who are going to be deciding

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:37.830
whether the company is going to option
it or not

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:41.280
that's fastening I wonder where there
are certain scripts that you would read

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:46.319
just the first 10 pages of and you just
knew right away this was not gonna go

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:50.280
anywhere and if yeah why I mean as crazy
as it sounds it could be the first

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:54.930
paragraph it could be the first line I
mean if there's two typos in the first

00:06:54.930 --> 00:07:01.669
line like it's probably not gonna go
anywhere you know it's very rare

00:07:01.669 --> 00:07:09.180
incredibly rare that the first 10 20 30
pages will be weak and then the script

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:16.139
gets a whole lot better later you know
if a writer doesn't know how to write a

00:07:16.139 --> 00:07:20.940
great act one they're not gonna learn
later or suddenly turn it around and

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:25.319
have a great act to a or act to be I
find this fascinating so I was just

00:07:25.319 --> 00:07:30.930
curious did you have a cubicle like your
own desk to really like digest this no

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:38.280
it was reading at home a reader is a
totally freelance job so at the time I

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:44.819
mean this really dates me a lot of the
work was actually in print so I would go

00:07:44.819 --> 00:07:49.050
to the company that I was working for
they would give me a script or two or a

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:55.259
stack or a book printed on paper I would
go home I would write up the coverage

00:07:55.259 --> 00:08:01.710
report and then I would actually really
early on I would put it on a disk on a

00:08:01.710 --> 00:08:06.120
floppy disk I would save it and then I
would come back in and give them the

00:08:06.120 --> 00:08:10.020
floppy disk and they would put it on
their computer into the server so that

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:15.060
the execs and president of the company
and stuff could access it you know but

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:19.860
yeah I was actually reading on paper now
they obviously just email a PDF and then

00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:24.479
it's up to the reader who works at home
whether they want to print it out and

00:08:24.479 --> 00:08:28.469
read it on paper or they want to read it
on their computer or tablet or whatever

00:08:28.469 --> 00:08:34.589
and what would you say was the average
amount of you know coverage that you

00:08:34.589 --> 00:08:38.699
were reading in a 24-hour period for one
thing like you said 600 pages for a book

00:08:38.699 --> 00:08:44.550
but that sounds like that maybe was
excessive it was yeah I when I was a

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:50.279
reader it I was in New York City so I
didn't read a ton of scripts at once

00:08:50.279 --> 00:08:54.840
like readers in LA
we'll come in and pick up or not pick up

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:59.070
these days but they'll get five or six
scripts at once and they'll have two or

00:08:59.070 --> 00:09:02.850
three days maybe four to turn them
around okay so they have to read all

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:06.480
these scripts right coverage reports and
send them back in to their employer

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.730
whether it's an agency or a production
company or a studio in my case I never

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:19.650
read that high of a volume but let's say
usually the turnaround was two days so

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:23.730
I'd get a script whether I got a script
or two or three let's say I got them on

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:29.040
Monday they would be due by Wednesday
but what was challenging and good

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:32.940
because it paid more money was the
overnight jobs so if they needed

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:37.440
something overnight I it would be
tougher because I would get less sleep

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:41.520
that night and I would really have to
blast this thing out and send it in in

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.810
the morning or let's say by noon if it
wasn't as urgent maybe by the end of the

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:50.190
day the next day but it would pay a
little bit more for overnight jobs so I

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:55.470
always liked those and so what did you
start to learn about really dissecting

00:09:55.470 --> 00:09:59.790
something because I mean are you like
speed reading how does one finish

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:05.190
something and then do coverage on it
within a two day period like that well

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:09.420
some readers might take notes as they go
they might write notes in the margins if

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:16.020
they're reading on paper but after a
while I just I just internalized my

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:22.140
thoughts and I actually had what I
called the brain dump which is for that

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:24.840
time period
of when I was focusing on this piece of

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:29.310
material whether 24 hours 48 hours I
would remember everything about it I

00:10:29.310 --> 00:10:34.080
mean I would be reading fast but I would
be absorbing everything right and I had

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:39.330
been doing this for a long time so I've
seen the patterns in structure and in

00:10:39.330 --> 00:10:45.470
writing and so I know immediately this
is a cliche or this page is too dense

00:10:45.470 --> 00:10:51.360
this dialogue is too on the nose
so I'm noting that as I'm reading really

00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:57.990
fast even though I'm reading fast I'm
catching these things and then when I'm

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:02.970
done I immediately would write up the
synopsis while it was fresh in my head

00:11:02.970 --> 00:11:07.529
and then I would write up my one page of
comments but the interesting thing was

00:11:07.529 --> 00:11:12.180
and the reason I call it the brain dump
was the next day or two days later a

00:11:12.180 --> 00:11:17.069
week later I didn't remember all that
detail because I was just my brain was

00:11:17.069 --> 00:11:21.389
focused on this one 48-hour period you
have to do coverage on this piece of

00:11:21.389 --> 00:11:27.509
material so keep it in your brain for 48
hours and then after that dump most of

00:11:27.509 --> 00:11:32.069
it you know I mean the great scripts and
the great books that I read on the job

00:11:32.069 --> 00:11:36.839
I still do remember you know elements of
them I mean I still remember lines from

00:11:36.839 --> 00:11:43.699
like The Sixth Sense script and from a
few other scripts and and some books but

00:11:43.699 --> 00:11:50.490
generally a reader is just evaluating it
for one read and it's in that one 24 to

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:54.990
48 hour period they're not doing pages
of pages of notes they're not meeting

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:59.339
with the writer you know this isn't
development they're just evaluating one

00:11:59.339 --> 00:12:04.110
read after it comes in the door and
they're handing their report in and then

00:12:04.110 --> 00:12:08.459
from there they don't really know where
it goes I'm just curious what were some

00:12:08.459 --> 00:12:13.939
common cliches at that time you were
seeing in these scripts well some big

00:12:13.939 --> 00:12:20.610
mistakes I would say that I would get in
scripts coming in the door were like big

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:26.459
really big dense blocks of description
just too many words on the page the

00:12:26.459 --> 00:12:30.209
worst is when they make the margins
really small so that they can fit more

00:12:30.209 --> 00:12:35.610
on the page because they haven't really
tightened the script up enough so with

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:41.790
making the margins tiny and and even the
spacing like the letting you can make

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:45.540
tighter and final draft and in other
screenwriting software's if you do that

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:50.790
the reader immediately knows that you're
cheating and they know that even your

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:55.500
script may say it's a hundred and ten
pages but if you went to normal margins

00:12:55.500 --> 00:13:00.870
and normal spacing it would probably be
like 123 or something so readers hate

00:13:00.870 --> 00:13:05.129
that you know right off the bat because
you're taking up more of their time and

00:13:05.129 --> 00:13:09.660
it also shows that you're an
undisciplined writer who doesn't know

00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:15.540
how to write a well-paced script in the
proper spacing and format that

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:23.070
into the average script being let's say
105 210 pages these days so there's that

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:28.889
and then there is takes too long to get
to the inciting incident the inciting

00:13:28.889 --> 00:13:34.680
incident should be between page eight to
ten sometimes twelve is okay but we

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:39.420
really need a catalyst you know we need
an inciting incident to happen and then

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:46.079
as well later on you don't want Act one
to be too far into the script it should

00:13:46.079 --> 00:13:52.079
be the the break of act 1 and act 2
should be probably around page 30 it

00:13:52.079 --> 00:13:55.589
doesn't have to be exactly on page 30
but around page 30 but some beginner

00:13:55.589 --> 00:14:00.660
scripts it literally be like page 47
before a really big turn and Direction

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:06.089
happened and it felt like we were in act
2 so those are some examples but the

00:14:06.089 --> 00:14:12.630
on-the-nose dialog could be one of the
worst I like to say sometimes that

00:14:12.630 --> 00:14:16.440
Talking Heads dialogue where it's just
two characters talking to each other

00:14:16.440 --> 00:14:20.430
kind of at each other and then Q&amp;A
sessions are the worst and that's where

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:23.970
one character keeps asking another
questions and then they just keep

00:14:23.970 --> 00:14:28.410
answering it so it's almost like an
interview and if that goes on for too

00:14:28.410 --> 00:14:32.910
long it really tires out the reader and
it just gets really what we call on the

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:37.500
nose Dan how many screenplays would you
say over the course of your lifetime

00:14:37.500 --> 00:14:43.529
you've read I probably over a thousand
I'm not one of those people who's like

00:14:43.529 --> 00:14:47.339
I've read five or six thousand dan of
the numerous scripts that you've read

00:14:47.339 --> 00:14:54.449
how many would you say went on to become
films very few it's very rare that they

00:14:54.449 --> 00:14:59.399
do it's hard for a script to get
optioned or sold and then it's even

00:14:59.399 --> 00:15:06.750
harder for it to get produced so it's
very rare and that's why I always say

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:10.709
your script has to be great it can't
just be good it has to be great that's

00:15:10.709 --> 00:15:15.569
why I titled my first book how to write
a great screenplay with great and in

00:15:15.569 --> 00:15:21.870
caps because working as a reader and now
as a writing coach something really has

00:15:21.870 --> 00:15:26.490
to blow me away and because it's so
tough for it to rise up the ladder and

00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:32.029
become an option script
old script a produced script so you

00:15:32.029 --> 00:15:36.170
really have to come in with something
with a unique voice with a unique

00:15:36.170 --> 00:15:42.769
approach to commercially successful
genre it's really tough so I can't

00:15:42.769 --> 00:15:47.630
really put my finger on how many became
produced of scripts that I read but it's

00:15:47.630 --> 00:15:53.899
few and far between so going back to
when you were you got a BFA at NYU yeah

00:15:53.899 --> 00:15:57.889
you were doing this like script reading
and all that from that time when you

00:15:57.889 --> 00:16:03.949
went to NYU to the beginning stages of
the script reading job were you aware of

00:16:03.949 --> 00:16:09.410
how tough it was or at that time it was
just like you thought that if something

00:16:09.410 --> 00:16:13.880
was really good and it was a first time
writer the doors were open I was

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:19.670
probably a bit more naive I probably
thought that more things rose up the

00:16:19.670 --> 00:16:26.480
ladder and got bought and greenlit and
produced but after a while I realized

00:16:26.480 --> 00:16:31.220
well first things first 9 out of 10
scripts I would pass on I would reject

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:36.920
so I got the sense right away that even
though this was you know on the

00:16:36.920 --> 00:16:40.880
professional level and these scripts
were coming from agents and managers and

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:47.269
producers some had major stars attached
but the material just wasn't there it

00:16:47.269 --> 00:16:54.769
just wasn't great and so I would pass on
most things 9 out of 10 things so what

00:16:54.769 --> 00:16:59.120
was great was as a reader I was always
looking for that thing to say yes to not

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:04.610
to say no to because it was so
exhilarating when I found that one out

00:17:04.610 --> 00:17:08.720
of 10 or one out of 20 scripts that
month that really blew me away and

00:17:08.720 --> 00:17:14.179
really brought it and excited me and I
was happy to give it a consider or

00:17:14.179 --> 00:17:19.220
recommend on the coverage report was it
that black or white was it either a yay

00:17:19.220 --> 00:17:24.439
or a nay or was there sometimes a middle
ground where yes but this needs to be

00:17:24.439 --> 00:17:28.940
tightened up yeah there's something
called consider with reservations which

00:17:28.940 --> 00:17:35.419
is this could be a great movie for my
employer but there's reservations about

00:17:35.419 --> 00:17:42.070
it it would be really expensive there's
not a kind of star role in it

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:47.320
it's too long so it need to be cut down
but we're considering it that reader is

00:17:47.320 --> 00:17:51.429
considering it because it's great
writing you know and now what's good

00:17:51.429 --> 00:17:57.730
about the consider with reservations or
just the consider level evaluation which

00:17:57.730 --> 00:18:01.210
because there's passed consider
recommend recommend is really high

00:18:01.210 --> 00:18:05.980
recommend is like this is not only great
material but this is ready and perfect

00:18:05.980 --> 00:18:11.070
for my employer so this particular
studio this is right in their wheelhouse

00:18:11.070 --> 00:18:15.820
it's right in the budget range so I
fully recommend that consider is a

00:18:15.820 --> 00:18:21.100
really good grade and any writer should
be excited to get a consider and what it

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:25.299
means is even if that particular piece
of material isn't right for that

00:18:25.299 --> 00:18:31.390
production company or that studio the
writer is a good writer and they may

00:18:31.390 --> 00:18:34.870
want to work with this writer on another
project so they may want to bring them

00:18:34.870 --> 00:18:40.480
